  "nameStory": "Northern St. John's-wort gets its name from its northern range and its membership in the St. John's-wort family, plants traditionally associated with St. John the Baptist. The family name comes from the European tradition of gathering these yellow-flowered plants around St. John's Day (June 24th), when many species reach peak bloom.",
  "description": "Northern St. John's-wort is a small, delicate perennial wildflower that rarely catches attention until you're looking closely. It grows as a short-lived plant, typically reaching just 6-18 inches tall with a slender, upright stem. Like its St. John's-wort relatives, it produces small yellow flowers, but this northern species is more modest and less showy than its common cousins. The plant has a rather sparse, open appearance with thin stems and small leaves.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Flowers",
      "description": "Small yellow flowers with 5 petals, much smaller and less conspicuous than common St. John's-wort, appearing almost delicate and papery when touched",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Leaves",
      "description": "Small, narrow leaves arranged opposite each other on the stem, feeling thin and somewhat fragile between your fingers",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Growth Habit",
      "description": "Slender, upright stems that feel surprisingly sturdy despite their thin appearance, typically unbranched or with few branches",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Fruit/Seeds",
      "description": "Tiny capsules that split open to release small seeds, much smaller than those of other St. John's-worts",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Northern St. John's-wort prefers cooler, northern habitats including moist meadows, bog edges, and areas around lakes and ponds. In New England, look for it in the more northern counties, particularly in areas with sandy or peaty soils that stay somewhat moist. It often grows alongside sedges and other plants that prefer cooler, wetter conditions.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "New growth emerges as small, paired leaves on thin reddish stems. Plants are quite inconspicuous at this stage.",
    "summer": "Small yellow flowers bloom from mid to late summer, much later than many of its St. John's-wort relatives. The delicate blooms are easily overlooked.",
    "fall": "Seed capsules mature and split open, while leaves may turn slightly reddish before the plant dies back. The thin stems become more brittle.",
    "winter": "As a short-lived perennial, plants die back completely, leaving no trace above ground until spring growth resumes."
  },
  "funFact": "Despite being called 'northern' St. John's-wort, this little plant is actually one of the rarest St. John's-worts in New England, with only 24 recorded observations across the entire region - making it more elusive than a needle in a haystack!",
  "confusionNotes": "Most easily confused with dwarf St. John's-wort, but northern St. John's-wort has a more northern range and prefers wetter habitats. Remember: 'Northern needs more moisture' - if you're in a dry field, you're probably looking at dwarf St. John's-wort instead.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "The rarity of northern St. John's-wort in New England means that any sighting is botanically significant. Many botanists go their entire careers without encountering this species, as it represents the southern edge of a primarily Canadian plant's range.",
  "compareHints": "Northern St. John's-wort is much smaller and more delicate than common St. John's-wort, lacking the robust growth and abundant flowers of that weedy species. Unlike dwarf St. John's-wort, which grows in dry sandy areas, northern St. John's-wort prefers moist conditions and has a more northern distribution. It's also less branched than Canada St. John's-wort and blooms later in the season than most of its relatives.",
